ment effects. The dataset highlights their role in AML and other cancers, including the concept that intensive chemotherapy can suppress counts and that counts are monitored for recovery over time. [Data: Entities (1783)]\\n\\nA specific relationship states that cancer can be a reason blood counts are too low or too high, anchoring blood-count abnormalities as a clinically meaningful indicator that can reflect malignancy (among other causes). This supports why blood counts are tracked throughout oncology treatment and follow-up as part of routine blood testing. [Data: Entities (1783); Relationships (3732)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Initial blood testing commonly includes chemistry profiles and may include tumor markers such as CEA in colon cancer workups\",\"explanation\":\"The community includes specific initial-test entities: CHEMISTRY PROFILE (a panel used to assess organ function and metabolic status) and CEA TEST (a tumor marker recommended as part of initial testing in colon cancer assessment). These entities sit under the broader BLOOD TESTS umbrella, indicating standardized panels and selective markers within initial evaluation workflows. [Data: Entities (4449, 4450, 156)]\\n\\nRelationships explicitly connect BLOOD TESTS to CHEMISTRY PROFILE and CEA TEST as recommended parts of initial blood testing. At the same time, the BLOOD TESTS description notes that markers like CEA are supportive and not used alone to diagnose colon cancer, placing tumor markers in an adjunctive role rather than a definitive diagnostic one. [Data: Entities (156, 4449, 4450); Relationships (9371, 9372)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Thyroid cancer surveillance relies on Tg measurement, but interpretation depends on thyroid tissue status and Tg antibodies\",\"explanation\":\"THYROGLOBULIN (TG) is described as a protein made only by thyroid tissue; after total thyroidectomy it should be absent from blood, and rising levels can indicate possible recurrence and prompt further testing. This positions Tg as a